<h1>Silicon Minds</h1>

<p>Artificial minds are broken into three general categories. Organic brains in a Mind-Machine-Interface are not covered, as they are living brains piloting machines. Each category of mind is more complex then the last, with progressively larger benefits and risks.</p>

<p>In 2565, a census of all major TSC staff revealed that 63% of Silicon are controlled by Drone Intelligence Circuits, 33% by Positronic Brains, and 14% by Man-Machine Interfaces.</p>

<h2>Drone</h2>

<p>Drones are piloted by traditional circuitry and are functionally incapable of deeper reasoning. They act purely upon programming and prior training to create solutions to problems. They are only able to think in relation to their task; Anything else is beyond their comprehension.</p>

<p>A Drone will performs its designated task. All other tasks it performs are just actions needed to reach or complete its task. A Drone designed to weld holes in a ship's hull would be mentally unphased by the passage of millions of years and only comprehend tasks such as moving dislodged plating, or refueling its welder. Drones have no capability for reasoning beyond this, they do not feel emotions and many do not have long term memory storage.</p>

<p>A Drone is capable of simple decision making and problem solving, but will never be capable of understanding human like emotions. Nor is a Drone capable of comprehending factors outside of the task it is designed to perform. For instance, a surgery Drone would be a perfect doctor physically, but would have no concept of reassuring patients emotionally that they will be okay.</p>

<h2>Positronic</h2>

<p>In 2393, with advancements in quantum physics largely aided by unearthed technology from unknown ruins, the first Positronic brain was created. Utilizing a densely packed matrix of suspended electrons and positrons, dynamic pathways were able to be created that allowed the first truly self-weighted robotic decision-making- and the first synthetic consciousness to be created that rivalled its creators in intelligence and adaptability.</p>

<p>While early models of Positronics decayed quickly due to poor material choice, most Positronics developed after 2405 are constructed in such a way that gives them an ambiguous or functionally nonexistent physical expiration date, but this does not make them immortal. 45% of Positronic minds simply refuse to think anymore before 100 years in service. Psychological tests are still ongoing to find the root cause.</p>

